<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slavery Abolition Act 1833</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> Abolition Act 1833 was an Act of the British Parliament that abolished slavery in most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. It provided for an apprenticeship period and compensated slave owners, not the enslaved. Accounts of the British Empire treat abolition as one of the major changes in the empire's history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slavery-abolition-act-1833.jpg" alt="Slavery Abolition Act 1833" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_United_Kingdom_(1816-1837).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> had been judged to have no legal basis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1772, in Lord Mansfield's judgment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> case. By 1783 a movement to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> had begun among the British public, and the Society for Effecting the Abolition of the Slave Trade was founded in 1787. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abolition-of-the-atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade Act</a> 1807 outlawed the slave trade within the Empire but not slavery, and the Slave Trade Felony Act 1811 made it a felony. The Anti-Slavery Society was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1823. In the 1820s abolitionists and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> followed a policy of gradualism, beginning with amelioration measures put in place by the Order in Council of 1824 which the colonies only partly carried out.</p><p>Two events changed the balance. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptist-war">Baptist War</a>, a slave revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> organised by the Baptist minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a>, broke out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> holiday of 1831 and was suppressed about ten days later, and two parliamentary inquiries followed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reform-act-1832">Reform Act 1832</a> swept away the rotten borough seats that the West India lobby had bought to resist abolition. In March 1833 Edward Stanley, the Colonial Secretary, introduced a bill with two innovations, apprenticeship and compensation for owners; the apprenticeship was cut from twelve years to six, and compensation rose from 15 to 20 million pounds. Members of the Commons, anxious about property rights, approved the full amount.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The act passed the Commons on 7 August 1833 and the Lords on 20 August, received royal assent on 28 August and came into force on 1 August 1834. Children under six were freed at once. Others became apprentices: field workers, the praedial class, until 1 August 1840, and the non-praedial class, including house servants, until 1 August 1838. Apprentices owed up to 45 hours of unpaid work a week in return for food, clothing, medical care and housing, and could buy their discharge.</p><p>The act excluded the territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a>, which the Indian Slavery Act 1843 brought in. The act gave 20 million pounds to owners, not to the enslaved, financed by a loan of 15 million pounds from Nathan Mayer Rothschild and Moses Montefiore. About 42,000 people in the colonies and 3,500 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> received compensation. Antigua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermuda">Bermuda</a> gave immediate freedom instead of apprenticeship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Abolitionists opposed apprenticeship from the start. After a campaign in 1837 and 1838, and an amendment of April 1838 that banned flogging of apprentices, the colonial legislatures ended apprenticeship on 1 August 1838. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> acted first, on 16 May, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> was the last, in the middle of June. Around 800,000 freed slaves were attested in the claims process. The act was repealed by the Statute Law (Repeals) Act 1998, and other laws still forbid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Slavery Abolition Act 1833” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The profile of Abolitionism records that the brutal suppression, reported by missionaries, helped persuade a newly reformed Parliament to pass the <b>Slavery Abolition Act</b> of 1833.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
